title: Transforming CoreMark

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/legacy-source/core_main.c
  - .assets/img-102-01-03.png
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/ActiveRunnerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-01-07.png

steps:
  - cmds: []
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]Transforming CoreMark}

      The prior **Basic Examples** tours featured small, single-file programs which introduced different facets of **EM&bull;Script**.

      Building upon this knowledge, we'll now dive into a larger program comprising multiple **EMS** application modules.

      This group of tours will specifically explore the widely-used **CoreMark&reg;** benchmark &ndash; re-written here in **EMS**.

      {[in]Starting with legacy C code, this exercise further rein&shy;forces <b>EMS</b> as a higher-level language alternative.}

      The initial tour in this group kicks-off a top-down explor&shy;ation of legacy CoreMark transformed through **EMS**.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 93]
    text: |
      {[ht,files]Legacy Sources}

      The CoreMark benchmark comprises six (portable) source files written in C and archived on {[le,https://github.com/eembc/coremark]GitHub}:

      {[bm,1]} `core_main.c` &ndash; benchmark execution harness<br>
      {[bm,2]} `coremark.h` &ndash; common declarations<br>
      {[bm,3]} `core_matrix.c` &ndash; matrix-math algorithm<br>
      {[bm,4]} `core_state.c` &ndash; state-machine algorithm<br>
      {[bm,5]} `core_list_join.c` &ndash; list-processing algorithm<br>
      {[bm,6]} `core_util.c` &ndash; CRC and seed-retrieval functions

      We'll peek into these C files only as a point of reference when exploring the corresponding **EMS** implementation.

      {[in]And in our opinion, not the prettiest C code either 😉}

  - cmds:
      - close 1
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,account_tree]EM&bull;Script Modules}

      This hierarchy of **EMS** modules implements the function&shy;ality of the legacy C code cataloged previously.

      The {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} program (our next stop) runs mul&shy;tiple benchmark iterations before printing results.

      Subsequent tours in this group cover the modules called directly/indirectly by {[cu]ActiveRunnerP}.

      The final tour covers {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} &ndash; which re-uses all of these modules in a very different application setting.

      {[in]%[cu]SleepRunnerP% leads us to <b>EM&bull;Mark</b> &ndash; an MCU bench&shy;mark favoring energy-efficiency over execution-time.}
  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 6
      - mark 3 B 11
      - mark 3 C 18
      - mark 3 D 25
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]ActiveRunnerP% &ndash; details}

      This program behaves similarly to `core_main.c` &ndash; execu&shy;ting multiple iterations of the benchmark algorithms.

      {[bm,A]} The {[cu]CoreBench} module manages the algorithms.<br>
      {[bm,B]} The {[cn]iterations} config parameter defaults to `10`.<br>
      {[bm,C]} {[cu]CoreBench}.{[cf]setup} preps the algorithms at startup.<br>
      {[bm,D]} {[cu]CoreBench}.{[cf]run}executes one algorithm iteration.<br>

      The next stop on this tour will inspect {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} output after executing ten CoreMark iterations.

      {[in]Unlike legacy CoreMark &ndash; which will often run for <i>ten seconds</i> &ndash; a small iteration count proves sufficient.}

  - cmds:
      - mark 3 A 29
    focus: [3, 1]
    acts: ["$build"]
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]ActiveRunner Output}

      TODO: Interpret the output that appears after building and running the program.

      {[bm,A]} TODO: Connect this printf block to the five terminal lines:

      {[cd]usecs = ...}
      {[cd]list crc = ...}
      {[cd]matrix crc = ...}
      {[cd]state crc = ...}
      {[cd]final crc = ...}

      TODO: Explain that {[cd]usecs} is a real elapsed-time measurement for this short configured run, while the CRCs are the correctness checks that keep the EMS rendering honest.

      TODO: Optionally mention the build size reported by the tools: larger than Basic Examples, still tiny for a real benchmark-style program.

  - cmds: []
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]Extra Credit}

      TODO: Suggest changing the iteration count from 10 to 50 or 100 and observing how elapsed time scales.

      TODO: Suggest browsing the legacy CoreMark C sources and comparing the visible C harness with the compact EMS runner.

      TODO: Suggest looking at the build output size and thinking about what would be required for a fair EMS-vs-C CoreMark comparison.

      TODO: Keep this stop light; do not turn it into a benchmark methodology lesson.

  - cmds:
      - close 3
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,$done]Next Steps}

      TODO: Return to the module map with {[cd]SleepyRunnerP} highlighted as the forward-looking bridge.

      TODO: Explain that this tour started with the active benchmark path, but the same {[cd]CoreBench} engine can later support a wake-work-sleep runner.

      TODO: Hand off to Tour 02: next we leave the roadmap and enter {[cd]CoreBench}, the shared benchmark engine behind {[cd]ActiveRunnerP}.
